AUDIO: 'everything else? Bitwarden\'s own self-hosted stack is real and properly supported, and self-hosting it is free, which surprises people. What isn\'t free is the license file. Premium and'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'organization features on your own server still need a license generated from a paid cloud plan. So, an unlicensed self-hosted Bitwarden is just the free tier running on your electricity. The standard deployment is 11 containers'

[02:27] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Slide titled "What else you'd run" with additional comparisons to 1Password and KeePass.
AUDIO: 'with a Microsoft SQL Server in the middle, though they shipped a much lighter single image built in December that genuinely narrows the gap. 1Password is the option most teams end up buying with deliberately no'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'self-hosting story at all. KeePass is the far end of the spectrum, where the vault is a file on your disk, and moving it between devices is entirely your problem. Proton Pass sits in the middle, hosted for you, trust handed to Proton.'

[02:54] [scene:stats]
VISUAL: Slide titled "Vaultwarden by the numbers" showing "66k Github Stars", "320M Docker Pulls", and "62% of public vault servers".
AUDIO: 'Four things on that card, and the star count is honestly the least interesting of them. What tells you it\'s actually running somewhere rather than sitting in a bookmark, is the pull count, though'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pulls aren\'t installs. The third number is the one that got me. When Censys went scanning for password manager interfaces exposed to the internet, this is what most of what they found was running. And'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the license is the reason nobody can quietly take this private. Right. So, what do you actually get? Bitwarden\'s premium tier was $10 a year until January, when it roughly doubled'

