[05:15] [scene:features]
VISUAL: Slide continuing the "per-seat tier" discussion, specifically mentioning SSO.
AUDIO: 'sign-on. Vaultwarden ships it over OpenID anyway after a pull request that sat open for 690 days, so the thing reserved for enterprise buyers is a config block on a box you already own.'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Underneath, it\'s built on the Rocket web framework, and the practical consequence of that choice is that the thing you deploy is one binary with no runtime to install next to it. Storage defaults to'

[05:41] [scene:tech_details]
VISUAL: Slide titled "Under the hood" detailing Vaultwarden's tech stack (Rocket, SQLite) with a screenshot of the Rocket web framework website.
AUDIO: 'SQLite, which is the right call for the overwhelming majority of people running this, and you can point it at Postgres or MySQL instead if you\'ve already got one, and you\'d rather not think about a'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second backup job. The web vault you see in the browser isn\'t the stock one. It\'s a patched build of Bitwarden\'s own client, maintained in a separate repo, and baked into the container image.'

[06:05] [scene:install]
VISUAL: Terminal showing Docker commands for pulling and running vaultwarden/server:latest.
AUDIO: 'Getting it running is close to trivial, and I suspect that\'s most of why it spread the way it did. You pull the image, then hand it a folder on the host to keep its data in, and the domain it\'s'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to answer on, and that\'s the deployment. Where people trip is the next part, because the web vault refuses to work over plain HTTP. Browsers only expose the web crypto API'

[06:30] [scene:security]
VISUAL: Slide titled "1.37.0 -> 1.37.2" discussing security fixes, with a screenshot of a GitHub release note for 1.37.0.
AUDIO: 'on a secure context, so there\'s a reverse proxy and a certificate in your future, whether you wanted one or not. The July release is the one to look at, because it disclosed a whole batch of'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'security advisories at once. Eight separate classes of bug, 10 advisory numbers between them. One was server-side request forgery through the icon endpoint, and another let a user'

[06:55] [scene:security]
VISUAL: Slide continuing the discussion on security fixes, detailing specific vulnerabilities.
AUDIO: 'read ciphers across organization boundaries. The nastiest is a websocket flood a client could fire without ever logging in. Enough to knock the whole server over. Every one of them rated'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'medium, nothing high or critical, and a month on, they\'re still private with no CVE numbers attached. That same release brought in trusted proxy support and rate limiting on unauthenticated'

[07:21] [scene:verdict]
VISUAL: Slide titled "Worth it?" with an illustration of a person on a platform being lifted by a crane.
AUDIO: 'endpoints, which are the two things you want in front of anything sitting on the open internet. Then 0.2 shipped two days ago, and that one is a compatibility bump, because the official clients'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moved, and the server had to move with them. The moment you run this, you\'re the operator, which means the backups are yours, and so is whatever happens the day it stops answering. The July batch isn\'t the criticism I\'d lead with'

[07:46] [scene:verdict]
VISUAL: Slide titled "Worth it?" with GitHub UI of Vaultwarden repo.
AUDIO: 'though. May is. Four advisories landed in the single sign-on code five months after it shipped. Two of them rated high, which is about what you\'d expect from a feature that sat in review for'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two years and then went in all at once. Balanced against that, this thing has actually been audited by Germany\'s Federal Cyber Security Agency and by an outside pen test team, which is more'

[08:10] [scene:verdict]
VISUAL: Slide titled "Worth it?" with GitHub UI of Vaultwarden repo.
AUDIO: 'scrutiny than most of what\'s running on your network has ever had. And the bit that stays with me is a line in that readme. The one about who signs one of the maintainers paychecks and how little anyone involved seems to mind. It'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reads less like a fork war and more like a company that decided this crowd was never the customer. If you already run a home server and you don\'t resent it, this earns its keep faster than almost anything else you'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could install. If that sentence made you tired, pay the subscription and sleep well.'
